(*
expect:
h1 100000
h2 100000
h3 100000
crash cycles 5000
restarts 5000
quarantined false
final 1
plateau true
done
*)
(* T-D.5 soak + leak plateau — the standing leak detector for the shared-heap
   tradeoff (a leaky job cannot be evicted by restart; creep must be caught in
   test, not in the field). Drives 100,000 iterations through three healthy
   handlers (300,000 firings total) with a periodic deliberate crash/restart
   cycle every 20th iteration (5,000 full crash->contain->restart cycles,
   never quarantining — see below), then proves the node is still alive and
   responsive with one final healthy event on a fresh channel. Slow by
   design; kept in the suite (no per-file slow-skip in the runner) at a count
   that is meaningful but does not dominate the gate — see MILESTONE_D.md.

   HEAP GAUGE (tuvm#24): `platform heapStats` (prim 104) reports
   liveBytes / highWater / gcCycles. highWater was REJECTED as the plateau
   gauge: GC in this VM fires only on arena exhaustion (no on-demand collect
   prim yet — filed as tuvm#35), so a healthy soak that never exhausts the
   arena never collects, and highWater is then a monotonic max over
   CUMULATIVE allocation (measured ~9.1 MB over this test's 90k-iteration
   window — pure noise, dwarfing any leak-sized signal). Raw liveBytes is no
   better between sweeps: it carries floating-garbage noise (live-at-last-
   sweep + every allocation since). The gauge that works: FORCE a sweep at
   each sample point — spin allocating droppable garbage until gcCycles
   ticks (see sweptLiveBytes:) — then read liveBytes at that sweep boundary,
   where it means true live data plus only the spin's own tail allocations.

   Why quarantine never triggers (so the crash/restart cycle is CONTINUOUS
   for the whole run, not just the first 3 crashes): each crash iteration
   posts, in order, `crasher` (crash) then `crasher/progress` (progress)
   before draining. FIFO dispatch within that one `drain` call fires
   `crasher` first — it crashes, and the crash record is appended to the
   BACK of the queue (behind the already-queued progress event) — then
   `progress` dequeues next and resets the job's crash counter to 0 via
   `noteProgress`, and only then does the crash record itself dequeue and
   `bumpCrashes` (0 -> 1, `restart`, since 1 < quarantineLimit 3). Every
   cycle repeats this 0->1->reset dance, so `crashes` never reaches 3 and
   `isQuarantined:` stays false for all 5,000 cycles — a real, repeated
   exercise of the restart path (sweep + re-register), not a one-shot. *)
class SoakLeak packageUsing: manifest = ( | h1Hits ::= 0. h2Hits ::= 0. h3Hits ::= 0.
                                            crashCycles ::= 0. restarts ::= 0. finalHits ::= 0.
                                            cycleCounter ::= 0. liveWarm ::= 0. | ) (
  (* Force a sweep, then read liveBytes. GC fires only on arena exhaustion
     (tuvm#35 tracks an on-demand collect prim), so spin allocating droppable
     garbage until the cycle counter ticks; the reading is then live data at
     a sweep boundary plus a few spin-tail allocations, which the plateau
     tolerance absorbs. Allocation is batched 64 per gcCycles poll so the
     heapStats free-bin walk is not paid per allocation. The spin cannot
     OOM: exhaustion IS the collection trigger, and the sweep frees the
     spin's own garbage. *)
  sweptLiveBytes: platform = (
    | before |
    before:: platform heapStats gcCycles.
    [ platform heapStats gcCycles = before ] whileTrue: [
      1 to: 64 do: [ :k | k printString , 'g' ] ].
    ^ platform heapStats liveBytes
  )
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space sup h1 h2 h3 crasher progress final n liveEnd |
    space:: platform space.
    sup::   platform supervisor.
    h1:: space channel: 'h1'.
    h2:: space channel: 'h2'.
    h3:: space channel: 'h3'.
    crasher::  space channel: 'crasher'.
    progress:: space channel: 'crasher/progress'.
    final::    space channel: 'final'.

    h1 whenever: '' do: [ :m | h1Hits:: h1Hits + 1 ].
    h2 whenever: '' do: [ :m | h2Hits:: h2Hits + 1 ].
    h3 whenever: '' do: [ :m | h3Hits:: h3Hits + 1 ].

    sup register: crasher
        restart: [ restarts:: restarts + 1.
                   crasher whenever: '' do: [ :m | self error: 'periodic soak crash' ] ]
        progress: progress.
    sup start.
    (* prime the crasher's first registration, same idiom as 05_restart_quarantine.ns *)
    crasher whenever: '' do: [ :m | self error: 'periodic soak crash' ].

    n:: 100000.
    1 to: n do: [ :i |
      h1 out: '' value: i.
      h2 out: '' value: i.
      h3 out: '' value: i.
      cycleCounter:: cycleCounter + 1.
      (cycleCounter = 20) ifTrue: [
        cycleCounter:: 0.
        crashCycles:: crashCycles + 1.
        crasher  out: '' value: i.
        progress out: '' value: i ].
      (i = 10000) ifTrue: [ liveWarm:: self sweptLiveBytes: platform ].
      space drain ].

    (* Node still alive and responsive: a fresh channel, registered only now,
       fires exactly once. *)
    final whenever: '' do: [ :m | finalHits:: finalHits + 1 ].
    final out: '' value: #ping.
    space drain.

    ('h1 ' , h1Hits printString) out.
    ('h2 ' , h2Hits printString) out.
    ('h3 ' , h3Hits printString) out.
    ('crash cycles ' , crashCycles printString) out.
    ('restarts ' , restarts printString) out.
    ('quarantined ' , (sup isQuarantined: 'crasher') printString) out.
    ('final ' , finalHits printString) out.

    (* T-D.5 STRICT PLATEAU (tuvm#24 — the assertion this test was designed
       around, enabled now that prim 104 exists): swept liveBytes is sampled
       at iteration 10,000 (past warm-up: caches, supervisor state, ring
       growth all settled) and again here, after 90,000 further iterations
       and ~4,500 further crash/restart cycles. Each sample is taken at a
       FORCED sweep boundary (see sweptLiveBytes: and the HEAP GAUGE note
       above for why highWater cannot serve), so floating garbage is out of
       the reading and any real leak — retained, so it survives the sweep —
       forces the delta upward. The delta may legitimately be negative (end
       live below warm-up live); <= handles that.
       TOLERANCE: 8192 bytes = max(4 x observed delta, 4096) rounded up to a
       power of two. Observed on host (2026-07-14, tuning per tuvm#24 Task 4):
       three runs, live delta 1240 / 1240 / 1240 bytes — this VM is
       deterministic, so cross-run noise is zero and the 1240 is the spin's
       own sweep-tail residue. 8192 is ~176x below the smallest leak this
       test must catch (16 B/iteration x 90k = 1.44 MB). Verdict only, never
       raw bytes (host/target/run vary). *)
    liveEnd:: self sweptLiveBytes: platform.
    ('plateau ' , ((liveEnd - liveWarm) <= 8192) printString) out.
    'done' out.
  )
) : (
)
